<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-17.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="000020"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000020" label="[00:00:20]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">As in, the live-bootstrap setup already has a script that takes care of building everything, wouldn't you be able to make a much smaller containerfile if you just used that?</span></div><div class="line" id="000029"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000029" label="[00:00:29]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Because various steps fail, or need investigation a lot, when we are customizing things</span></div><div class="line" id="000035"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000035" label="[00:00:35]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then you have to start from 0</span></div><div class="line" id="000047"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000047" label="[00:00:47]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">by having every step on a dedicated RUN line, we can always pick up from where we failed clean</span></div><div class="line" id="000110"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000110" label="[00:01:10]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is a massive development/experimentation boon, but also much easier to audit without all the plumbing in the way that we have to reason about</span></div><div class="line" id="000126"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000126" label="[00:01:26]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I see, that makes sense.</span></div><div class="line" id="000143"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000143" label="[00:01:43]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Though, wouldn't the ADD instruction near the top invalidate all other layers, every time anything in the steps changes?</span></div><div class="line" id="000304"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000304" label="[00:03:04]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yes it would, though for now I am not touching the steps themselves and just overriding things in place at lower levels with env vars and heredocs to override things in place at the steps I need to experiment</span></div><div class="line" id="000315"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000315" label="[00:03:15]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then when a pattern works, I can go adjust the base for a clean end result</span></div><div class="line" id="000315"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000315" label="[00:03:15]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It'd be really cool to get something like this useful for development, the current setup with packing and extracting packages works but is a bit finnicky.</span></div><div class="line" id="000342"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000342" label="[00:03:42]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah, that makes sense for a workflow yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="000358"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000358" label="[00:03:58]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess you could also insert an ADD to update a specific step's files in the middle there</span></div><div class="line" id="000417"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000417" label="[00:04:17]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah exactly. done that in a couple spots too. like just-in-time patches</span></div><div class="line" id="000542"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000542" label="[00:05:42]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what I ultimately hope to do is one step beyond that example, and just export a folder of scripts and a Containerfile that are standalone and do not require live-bootstrap, configurator, or script-generator at all that bases on an existing stage0 build like we have in stagex</span></div><div class="line" id="000611"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000611" label="[00:06:11]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what's stagex</span></div><div class="line" id="000613"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000613" label="[00:06:13]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at that point we end up with a super easy to audit/debug setup, but we can regenerate it if there are any significant developments in live-bootstrap upstream</span></div><div class="line" id="000624"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000624" label="[00:06:24]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex">https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="000642"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000642" label="[00:06:42]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a container-native full-source-bootstrapped deterministic and multi-signed toolchain to build all the things</span></div><div class="line" id="000723"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000723" label="[00:07:23]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">based on stage0, and then partially live-bootsrap for our stage0 and stage1 container images, then we pivot to a cross compiler container in stage2, and native in stage3, and build everything else all the way up to rustc and beyond from there, which is &quot;stageax&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="000740"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000740" label="[00:07:40]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;stagex&quot;*</span></div><div class="line" id="000854"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000854" label="[00:08:54]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/branch/lance/bootstrap-overhaul/packages/bootstrap/stage0/Containerfile">https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/branch/lance/bootstrap-overhaul/packages/bootstrap/stage0/Containerfile</a>  our current stage0 approach for reference</span></div><div class="line" id="000911"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000911" label="[00:09:11]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then deriving a new &quot;stage1&quot; based on live-bootstrap right now</span></div><div class="line" id="000928"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000928" label="[00:09:28]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whole distro works on our previously super hacky one, but making a leaner and easier to audit replacement now</span></div><div class="line" id="001049"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001049" label="[00:10:49]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since OCI images do not even need docker to build these days, and can jsut use shell scripts, they make for a portable standard for staged builds like bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="001119"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001119" label="[00:11:19]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but you get nice output caching when you happen to be using dev toolchains like docker</span></div><div class="line" id="001227"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001227" label="[00:12:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or kaniko, or buildkit, or podman, etc</span></div><div class="line" id="001240"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001240" label="[00:12:40]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whole goal here is toolchain diversity</span></div><div class="line" id="001240"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001240" label="[00:12:40]">&lt;civodul&gt;</a></span><span class="message">uh, interesting</span></div><div class="line" id="001336"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001336" label="[00:13:36]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sorry for taking a bit to reply</span></div><div class="line" id="001347"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001347" label="[00:13:47]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">was reading the stagex code a little</span></div><div class="line" id="001413"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001413" label="[00:14:13]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">really cool setup</span></div><div class="line" id="001658"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001658" label="[00:16:58]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Thanks! We are basically a drop-in replacement for debian, alpine, etc in basically every containerized build process deployed in the wild today.</span></div><div class="line" id="001744"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001744" label="[00:17:44]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm just wondering what the approach is towards dependency resolution, unless there isn't any?</span></div><div class="line" id="001809"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001809" label="[00:18:09]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you mean package management?</span></div><div class="line" id="001847"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001847" label="[00:18:47]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well classically in containerized builds, people would just &quot;apt install gcc&quot; or something, which is non deterministic and means your builds will vary from one day to the next.</span></div><div class="line" id="001847"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001847" label="[00:18:47]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean how do you decide what is built in what order</span></div><div class="line" id="001855"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001855" label="[00:18:55]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in packages/core</span></div><div class="line" id="001912"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001912" label="[00:19:12]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh. Our thinking there was just use the tools people already have installed</span></div><div class="line" id="001916"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001916" label="[00:19:16]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so the current solution is just make</span></div><div class="line" id="001930"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001930" label="[00:19:30]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">targets.py generates makefiles, then make can handle build order</span></div><div class="line" id="001950"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001950" label="[00:19:50]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">using docker, or a shell script based OCI runtime, or buildah, etc</span></div><div class="line" id="002005"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002005" label="[00:20:05]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we feel the more options we can implement the better</span></div><div class="line" id="002010"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002010" label="[00:20:10]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ohhh</span></div><div class="line" id="002043"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002043" label="[00:20:43]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah it's interpreting the COPY commands</span></div><div class="line" id="002055"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002055" label="[00:20:55]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah crude, but easy to audit</span></div><div class="line" id="002114"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002114" label="[00:21:14]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">most decisions we made were about auditability over elegance</span></div><div class="line" id="002218"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002218" label="[00:22:18]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">heh</span></div><div class="line" id="002305"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002305" label="[00:23:05]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and we also are bootstrapping a binary &quot;sxctl&quot; which we plan to be a standalone builder that can read from the packages directory and build anything in it, with only a basic kernel as the only dependency. Then we can build the whole tree baremetal in addition to being compatible with all containerized build systems.</span></div><div class="line" id="002430"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002430" label="[00:24:30]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I generally tend to forgo containerfiles/dockerfiles in favor of just using shell scripts, keeping each step &quot;manageable&quot; time-wise so I don't have to think about layers as much.</span></div><div class="line" id="002431"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002431" label="[00:24:31]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Here is what a deterministic full bootable OS looks like based on stagex: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.distrust.co/public/airgap/src/branch/main/Containerfile">https://git.distrust.co/public/airgap/src/branch/main/Containerfile</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="002439"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002439" label="[00:24:39]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But you really went all the way and that's really cool.</span></div><div class="line" id="002440"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002440" label="[00:24:40]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">very explicit, but that is the point.</span></div><div class="line" id="002527"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002527" label="[00:25:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can build and get all the same digests we do at the top of the file, and rapidly form confidence we did not tamper with the final airgapos iso, which in turn is used for high trust key generation ceremonies etc</span></div><div class="line" id="002534"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002534" label="[00:25:34]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh lord that's massive lol</span></div><div class="line" id="002655"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002655" label="[00:26:55]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we want to add a &quot;lock&quot; feature to sxctl that grabs the hashes from the lates release, verifies they have been signed/reproduced by 2+ maintainer keys you trust, and then automatically edit the Containerfile to pin the new hashes</span></div><div class="line" id="002756"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002756" label="[00:27:56]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">wait, for some reason I thought hashes wouldn't be reproducible because you don't preserve the entire graph of images used to build one</span></div><div class="line" id="002838"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002838" label="[00:28:38]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">all our digests are deterministic, and match what is in the &quot;digests&quot; folder in the stagex tree</span></div><div class="line" id="002901"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002901" label="[00:29:01]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you can run &quot;make&quot; in the stagex tree in main, and you will get all the same digests from your own local build of the packages directory as what is pinned in this airgapos file</span></div><div class="line" id="002933"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002933" label="[00:29:33]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Nice!</span></div><div class="line" id="002948"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002948" label="[00:29:48]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Docker does not allow this as they have weird metadata nonsense, but if you force docker to build OCI spec compliant images (recently supported) or use any other OCI compatible builder, they match</span></div><div class="line" id="003008"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003008" label="[00:30:08]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, so that's the magic</span></div><div class="line" id="003040"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003040" label="[00:30:40]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah I also don't expect the hashes to *always* change when the hash of an input changes</span></div><div class="line" id="003051"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003051" label="[00:30:51]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's what nixos does and for some reason that confused me a bit</span></div><div class="line" id="003054"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003054" label="[00:30:54]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you just tell docker to use the &quot;containerd-snapshotter&quot; backend (OCI) and then all the intermediates are deterministic as long as your actual build commands are deterministic.</span></div><div class="line" id="003138"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003138" label="[00:31:38]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I see</span></div><div class="line" id="003202"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003202" label="[00:32:02]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/jirutka/sloci-image">https://github.com/jirutka/sloci-image</a>  is also an option, though our makefiles do not use it, yet</span></div><div class="line" id="003259"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003259" label="[00:32:59]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sloci-image would be easy to support very very early in bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="003312"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003312" label="[00:33:12]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is no reason live-bootstrap could not support it, and then live-bootstrap images could in theory build stagex directly too</span></div><div class="line" id="003330"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003330" label="[00:33:30]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just need a posix shell</span></div><div class="line" id="003358"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003358" label="[00:33:58]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Thus live-bootstrap -could- build all the way up to rustc by pivoting to sloci-image to bridge to stagex</span></div><div class="line" id="003447"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003447" label="[00:34:47]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But yeah, nixos and guix did not fit our threat model, so were kind of forced to start over</span></div><div class="line" id="003521"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003521" label="[00:35:21]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and thankfully we did it at a time when the OCI standard can well cover all the package management needs so we don't have to roll yet another new packaging standard</span></div><div class="line" id="003625"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003625" label="[00:36:25]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that makes a lot of sense, especially to roll small container images, as most of the distros, even the reproducible ones, tend to include a lot of stuff only expected on a desktop system</span></div><div class="line" id="003710"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003710" label="[00:37:10]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yes, and all of them have points of trust in single maintainers, or things that are not actually reproducible.</span></div><div class="line" id="003742"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003742" label="[00:37:42]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like they -say- qemu is reproducible in some of these distros, but they do not actually build the firmware etc from source, which we do.</span></div><div class="line" id="003753"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003753" label="[00:37:53]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh like that</span></div><div class="line" id="003801"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003801" label="[00:38:01]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah qemu is a bit of an &quot;eh&quot; problem</span></div><div class="line" id="003827"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003827" label="[00:38:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we actually full source bootstrapped it with all firmware, because we need it in our build nodes</span></div><div class="line" id="003838"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003838" label="[00:38:38]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since we run all builds in throw-away VMs in our CI</span></div><div class="line" id="003905"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003905" label="[00:39:05]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">next stop we want to be able to prove the CI systems have not been tampered with, so we need a remote attestation solution that will work on many different hardware vendors</span></div><div class="line" id="003930"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003930" label="[00:39:30]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">TPM2 attestation fits the bill, but that requires trust in the system firmware to not lie about PCRs</span></div><div class="line" id="003935"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003935" label="[00:39:35]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so how do you ensure full reproducibility</span></div><div class="line" id="004009"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#004009" label="[00:40:09]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because as far as I'm aware you kinda have the same problem as guix where it's a best effort based on compiling everything on as many machines as possible</span></div><div class="line" id="004012"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004012" label="[00:40:12]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it is currently not possible to reproduce coreboot without an ada compiler... so currently working on bootstrapping ada, then we are finally able to go all the way down to firmware and tpm2 trust</span></div><div class="line" id="004051"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#004051" label="[00:40:51]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lol</span></div><div class="line" id="004055"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004055" label="[00:40:55]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mkid-kid: We actually can not release unless multiple maintiners build and get identical results</span></div><div class="line" id="004114"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004114" label="[00:41:14]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but our &quot;repros&quot; OS, also bootstrapped from stagex, is a potential answer to this</span></div><div class="line" id="004117"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#004117" label="[00:41:17]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's an insane amount of effort, really impressive</span></div><div class="line" id="004134"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004134" label="[00:41:34]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">where lots of people deploy ReprOS nodes that automatically build sign reproductions of every PR</span></div><div class="line" id="004155"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004155" label="[00:41:55]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and we cannot merge until N signatures from maintainer-run ReprOS machines with remote attestation proofs all land</span></div><div class="line" id="004210"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#004210" label="[00:42:10]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">docker, virtual machines, etc... all this implies huge dependency graphs and a circular dependency with regards to bootstrapping; may not be a hindrance to elevate trust and reproducibility, but it is a hindrance to bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="004211"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004211" label="[00:42:11]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that is our plan to get to rolling releases without trusting any single maintainer or hardware vendor</span></div><div class="line" id="004254"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004254" label="[00:42:54]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: docker is optional, but a major quaility of live improvement for development</span></div><div class="line" id="004309"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004309" label="[00:43:09]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: you can build anything in the stagex tree with a posix shell script too</span></div><div class="line" id="004315"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004315" label="[00:43:15]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that is why compatibility is a win</span></div><div class="line" id="004426"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004426" label="[00:44:26]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">standards mean people can build and develop the same core packages under different threat models. Some contributors can use docker on macs, while others will build only from a baremetal microkernel.</span></div><div class="line" id="004435"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004435" label="[00:44:35]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">more diversity the better</span></div><div class="line" id="004550"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004550" label="[00:45:50]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">once we have more people helping with reproduction, we can even mandate certian diversity charitaristics. Some building from baremetal microkernels, some in remote attested cloud VMs using ReprOS, some building on workstations, and in a diversity of legal jurisdictions</span></div><div class="line" id="004557"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004557" label="[00:45:57]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we want to make it easy to keep stacking trust</span></div><div class="line" id="005609"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005609" label="[00:56:09]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Boo. Made it all the way to autogen-5.18.16 before it died this time. <a rel="nofollow" href="https://dpaste.org/f8KC1">https://dpaste.org/f8KC1</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="005622"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005622" label="[00:56:22]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">must be more env magic I did not account for</span></div><div class="line" id="010313"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010313" label="[01:03:13]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The fact it is failing this deep makes me think this some sort of a bug that is being swallowed today some other way</span></div><div class="line" id="010343"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010343" label="[01:03:43]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because chroot build works from the same head</span></div><div class="line" id="010523"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010523" label="[01:05:23]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though 2.sh -does- run with &quot;set -e&quot;. hmm</span></div><div class="line" id="010609"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010609" label="[01:06:09]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">What about autogen-5.18.16 is unlike anything that came before it pattern wise...</span></div><div class="line" id="011146"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#011146" label="[01:11:46]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Here is the full Containerfile I am using, which is dropped right into the root of live-bootstap master: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://termbin.com/z616">https://termbin.com/z616</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="011201"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#011201" label="[01:12:01]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which failes at autogen-5.18.16 as commented</span></div><div class="line" id="011210"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#011210" label="[01:12:10]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Maybe someone knows of a hack or two going on there that may be burning me</span></div><div class="line" id="011359"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#011359" label="[01:13:59]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">looks like append-reverse is a feature that was added in mes-0.27</span></div><div class="line" id="012342"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#012342" label="[01:23:42]">&lt;stikonas_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, but for autogen you don't use mes...</span></div><div class="line" id="012348"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#012348" label="[01:23:48]">&lt;stikonas_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">proper guile is used</span></div><div class="line" id="012427"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012427" label="[01:24:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But yet it is complaining about append-reverse missing. Hrm</span></div><div class="line" id="012429"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#012429" label="[01:24:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if it somehow picked up mes, then failure is not unexpected</span></div><div class="line" id="012453"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#012453" label="[01:24:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh you have GUILE_LOAD_PATH set</span></div><div class="line" id="012459"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#012459" label="[01:24:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to mes directory</span></div><div class="line" id="012527"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012527" label="[01:25:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Oh. so it is always injecting mes stuff, and this just happens to be the first spot it burned me</span></div><div class="line" id="012533"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012533" label="[01:25:33]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at what point should I be unsetting that?</span></div><div class="line" id="012555"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#012555" label="[01:25:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">once tcc is built</span></div><div class="line" id="012606"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#012606" label="[01:26:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(for the first time)</span></div><div class="line" id="012657"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012657" label="[01:26:57]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Must have missed that since my setup forces me to bypass update_env.sh</span></div><div class="line" id="012709"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012709" label="[01:27:09]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Thanks!</span></div><div class="line" id="012711"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012711" label="[01:27:11]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION tries</span></div><div class="line" id="012720"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012720" label="[01:27:20]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">You probably just saved me several hours</span></div><div class="line" id="012817"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012817" label="[01:28:17]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it seems to have made it further. Good sign</span></div><div class="line" id="012936"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012936" label="[01:29:36]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Once this builds I get to run it again and find out why hashes do not match what is stored in tree sometimes. Probably other env var goofs</span></div><div class="line" id="013119"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#013119" label="[01:31:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, could be</span></div><div class="line" id="013131"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013131" label="[01:31:31]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we made it to python. very good sign.</span></div><div class="line" id="013132"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#013132" label="[01:31:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap does try to keep environment reasonably clean</span></div><div class="line" id="013149"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#013149" label="[01:31:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not really possible in early stages before bash</span></div><div class="line" id="013215"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#013215" label="[01:32:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but often later we clean environment by using exec env -i bash...</span></div><div class="line" id="013227"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013227" label="[01:32:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well and in a Container every line is a standalone shell that only inherits whatever env was explicitly above it</span></div><div class="line" id="013236"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013236" label="[01:32:36]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which will make it very easy to reason about the state of the env once I get this working</span></div><div class="line" id="013248"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013248" label="[01:32:48]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but currently it is hard to reason about all the script-based env mutation</span></div><div class="line" id="013354"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013354" label="[01:33:54]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Maybe I inject an &quot;env&quot; command early and have it print the state of the env after every step and when it differs from a previous step so I can adjust the env in my containerfile at that step ^_^</span></div><div class="line" id="013544"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013544" label="[01:35:44]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Also is there a reason FINAL_JOBS is hardcoded to 2? Is there some determinism factor here?</span></div><div class="line" id="013612"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013612" label="[01:36:12]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I know nproc is probably not available, but no reason I can't do JOBS=$((${$(&lt;/sys/devices/system/cpu/possible)/0-/} + 1)) right?</span></div><div class="line" id="014007"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014007" label="[01:40:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">depending on when this is done, fiwix (or linux2.4) haven't got sysfs</span></div><div class="line" id="014058"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014058" label="[01:40:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">minor side note, included JFS support with linux2.4; it's working, it's stable, it's easily done, and give a little more headroom until year2106 with posix32 kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="014156"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014156" label="[01:41:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's just, i cannot backport nilfs2 to linux2.4, too much hazzle, and i need to migrate all my storage to another filesystem since i intend to move my development host back onto linux2.4 for extensive testing</span></div><div class="line" id="014247"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014247" label="[01:42:47]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">worst case we get a super bare bones &quot;nproc&quot; in mescc-tools-extra or something</span></div><div class="line" id="014311"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014311" label="[01:43:11]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that would probably be the most portable solution to allow making use of more cores earlier in the build process</span></div><div class="line" id="014556"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014556" label="[01:45:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: it's just defaults to 2</span></div><div class="line" id="014607"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014607" label="[01:46:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap I think can override it using config file</span></div><div class="line" id="014645"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014645" label="[01:46:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and the reason to keep it a bit more controlled manually is to be able to run in low memory requirements</span></div><div class="line" id="014658"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014658" label="[01:46:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can stil set it to high core count manually</span></div><div class="line" id="014729"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014729" label="[01:47:29]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It seems it does not even switch above 1 core until the build of musl 1.2.4</span></div><div class="line" id="014744"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014744" label="[01:47:44]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but is there any reason in a container or chroot to not let it use all available cores for the whole build?</span></div><div class="line" id="014753"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014753" label="[01:47:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's fiwix run I think</span></div><div class="line" id="014759"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014759" label="[01:47:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in container you can use more</span></div><div class="line" id="014831"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014831" label="[01:48:31]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even with rootfs.py chroot setup, it uses 1 core until musl 1.2.4. Thought maybe that was a determinisim workaround</span></div><div class="line" id="014839"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014839" label="[01:48:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I doubt it</span></div><div class="line" id="014840"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014840" label="[01:48:40]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">comment says SMP support, but not relevant if you are already running from a modern kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="014857"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014857" label="[01:48:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, at least I don't remember the reason</span></div><div class="line" id="014907"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#014907" label="[01:49:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe fossy or Googulator know something</span></div><div class="line" id="014941"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014941" label="[01:49:41]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But okay, so I hardcode to 1, and switch to 2 as soon as I have bash and can run that one liner above to parse /sys/devices/system/cpu/possible</span></div><div class="line" id="014950"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014950" label="[01:49:50]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and see what explodes</span></div><div class="line" id="015027"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015027" label="[01:50:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like 99% of the time we are working on this we are building in containers or chroots with 20+ cores and waiting for half a day so this would be a major productivity win</span></div><div class="line" id="015229"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#015229" label="[01:52:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">apropos, SMP seems stable now too with linux2.4/tinycc, and this system can bootstrap full GNU wherever an i586-tcc-linux-musl.iso can boot</span></div><div class="line" id="015340"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#015340" label="[01:53:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i want to do extensive testing before, hence i'm glad i got a production filesystem with linux-2.4 which may not need any update soon</span></div><div class="line" id="015533"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#015533" label="[01:55:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the devdrop ISO is 450MiB in size currently with the complete package set supported (can be easily reduced in size but i want to keep test-coverage for tinycc)</span></div><div class="line" id="015620"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015620" label="[01:56:20]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">now that I think about it, even if I did have JOBS &gt; 1, until we have GCC, nothing can really take advantage of multiple cores anyway right?</span></div><div class="line" id="015631"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015631" label="[01:56:31]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and we have coreutils 5.0 before we get gcc</span></div><div class="line" id="015644"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015644" label="[01:56:44]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which should have nproc already</span></div><div class="line" id="015710"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015710" label="[01:57:10]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so could just switch JOBS to whatever nproc --all gives us after coreutils is built</span></div><div class="line" id="020029"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020029" label="[02:00:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, tinycc is very fast, although it too benefits from parallel make</span></div><div class="line" id="020311"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020311" label="[02:03:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">didn't include coreutils myself, and chose busybox plus extra utils (jfs, iptables whatever)</span></div><div class="line" id="020437"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020437" label="[02:04:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">keeping in mind i rather avoided autotools with as many packages as possible, ideally a system profile can be tailored to bootstrap a complete system with development utilities without relying on autotools too early during boostrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="020541"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020541" label="[02:05:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think it's even possible to avoid binutils, if i rewrite the bootcode assembly parts of linux-2.4 (it isn't many)</span></div><div class="line" id="020553"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020553" label="[02:05:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to assemble those with as86 instead</span></div><div class="line" id="020614"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020614" label="[02:06:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as86 itself may be easier to bootstrap too</span></div><div class="line" id="020823"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#020823" label="[02:08:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: GCC is single threaded, it's nothing to do with gcc</span></div><div class="line" id="020835"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#020835" label="[02:08:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's make that parallelizes stuff</span></div><div class="line" id="021141"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021141" label="[02:11:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some piece is missing still, since i need some networking filesystem; linux-2.4 got some shfs patchset which i'll try tomorrow</span></div><div class="line" id="021219"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021219" label="[02:12:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the beauty of linux2.4 in this case, it can be fully bootstrapped with tinycc, and provide rather useful features early in the dependency chains</span></div><div class="line" id="021237"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021237" label="[02:12:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">kernel-side</span></div><div class="line" id="021422"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021422" label="[02:14:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and tccboot is another decent approach to fill a gap with kernel bootstrapping (that might rely on the mentioned bootcode rewrite for as86)</span></div><div class="line" id="021632"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021632" label="[02:16:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the fiwix-kexec approach got an advantage over my approach still... it's ready and working, while i could not fully clarify yet, how much of the userspace stuff could be salvaged _before_ fiwix abi is required while bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="022426"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#022426" label="[02:24:26]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: ah right, so actually it -would- make sense to have the total number of cores in a var before make is built in the early stages. Fair</span></div><div class="line" id="022519"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#022519" label="[02:25:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think MAKEOPTS is that var</span></div><div class="line" id="022701"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#022701" label="[02:27:01]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Okay so then calculating the number of cores dynamically with mes or tinycc before the first version of make is built might make sense.</span></div><div class="line" id="022742"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#022742" label="[02:27:42]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a tiny bit of c or scheme to parse /sys/devices/system/cpu/possible seems like the shortest path at that stage</span></div><div class="line" id="022746"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#022746" label="[02:27:46]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unless anyone else has better ideas</span></div><div class="line" id="022859"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#022859" label="[02:28:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, maybe I'm wrong, maybe only --jobs work</span></div><div class="line" id="022934"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#022934" label="[02:29:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is MAKEFLAGS...</span></div><div class="line" id="022941"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#022941" label="[02:29:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.gnu.org/software/make/manual/html_node/Options_002fRecursion.html">https://www.gnu.org/software/make/manual/html_node/Options_002fRecursion.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="023238"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023238" label="[02:32:38]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as best I can tell make has no built in way to detect cores on the fly</span></div><div class="line" id="023417"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023417" label="[02:34:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no, it does not do that automatically</span></div><div class="line" id="023435"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023435" label="[02:34:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's configuration on invocation either with -j or -l</span></div><div class="line" id="023443"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023443" label="[02:34:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(these are not the same though)</span></div><div class="line" id="023531"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023531" label="[02:35:31]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah okay, so shortest path sounds like writing cpunum binary in .c that can be built with M2Libc/tcc ?</span></div><div class="line" id="023639"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023639" label="[02:36:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, but steps between make and coreutils are fast</span></div><div class="line" id="023650"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023650" label="[02:36:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you'll be just wasting your dev effor for little benefit</span></div><div class="line" id="023719"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023719" label="[02:37:19]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah, fair enough. What matters most is that we have it before gcc, which is expensive</span></div><div class="line" id="023728"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023728" label="[02:37:28]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean the most expensive thing is mes, but it seems there is no helping that noe</span></div><div class="line" id="023755"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023755" label="[02:37:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, if you build mes with kaem</span></div><div class="line" id="023759"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023759" label="[02:37:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then yes, no parallelization</span></div><div class="line" id="023806"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023806" label="[02:38:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: you could speed up mes in another wya</span></div><div class="line" id="023819"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023819" label="[02:38:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(which can be upstreamed to live-bootstrap too)</span></div><div class="line" id="023833"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023833" label="[02:38:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">create unified-libc file rather than building each little .c file one by one</span></div><div class="line" id="023856"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023856" label="[02:38:56]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah we bootstrap from hex0 to mescc-tools-extra in our &quot;stage0&quot; container, then we have our own Containerfile runner for live-bootstrap as stage1</span></div><div class="line" id="023907"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023907" label="[02:39:07]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but we can override anything in live-bootstrap easily now</span></div><div class="line" id="023926"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023926" label="[02:39:26]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: I do not follow</span></div><div class="line" id="023955"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#023955" label="[02:39:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">see this <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/15cb1064ee9cc54f880586fb814752c459a5c897/steps/tcc-0.9.26/pass1.kaem#L99">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/15cb1064ee9cc54f880586fb814752c459a5c897/steps/tcc-0.9.26/pass1.kaem#L99</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="024004"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024004" label="[02:40:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we concatenate meslibc files into one</span></div><div class="line" id="024018"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024018" label="[02:40:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then just do <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/15cb1064ee9cc54f880586fb814752c459a5c897/steps/tcc-0.9.26/pass1.kaem#L116">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/15cb1064ee9cc54f880586fb814752c459a5c897/steps/tcc-0.9.26/pass1.kaem#L116</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="024020"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024020" label="[02:40:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that's with tcc</span></div><div class="line" id="024044"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024044" label="[02:40:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but with mes we did <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/15cb1064ee9cc54f880586fb814752c459a5c897/steps/mes-0.27/pass1.kaem#L117">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/15cb1064ee9cc54f880586fb814752c459a5c897/steps/mes-0.27/pass1.kaem#L117</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="024103"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024103" label="[02:41:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and so lots of time is spend loading mescc over and over again</span></div><div class="line" id="024120"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024120" label="[02:41:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">s/spend/spent/</span></div><div class="line" id="024132"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024132" label="[02:41:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and loading mescc is not that cheap</span></div><div class="line" id="024238"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#024238" label="[02:42:38]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Okay so you are thinking there is no reason to not do that same hack for the mes build which might save us some signifiant time on that step</span></div><div class="line" id="024707"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024707" label="[02:47:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, there might be increase in memory usage</span></div><div class="line" id="024718"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024718" label="[02:47:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hard to tell how much exactly without trying</span></div><div class="line" id="025815"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025815" label="[02:58:15]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">great call. left a comment to try that on my next pass</span></div><div class="line" id="025827"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025827" label="[02:58:27]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">will see how much JOBS=$(nproc) saves us</span></div><div class="line" id="042453"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#042453" label="[04:24:53]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hi, I have some progress trying to bootstrap microhs from hugs, currently compile-time errors are fixed, but runtime is broken, I hope someone can continue from here, because this is as far as I can go <a rel="nofollow" href="https://0.vern.cc/Mm.patch">https://0.vern.cc/Mm.patch</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="181949"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#181949" label="[18:19:49]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Got almost all of live-bootstrap to build with auto-detected JOBS=20</span></div><div class="line" id="181952"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#181952" label="[18:19:52]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is -much- faster</span></div><div class="line" id="182046"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#182046" label="[18:20:46]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">~2h vs ~10+</span></div><div class="line" id="182903"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#182903" label="[18:29:03]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">This setup fully builds live-bootstrap master, skipping all plumbing and scripts not relevant for containers, with caching for each layer to hack on any step <a rel="nofollow" href="https://termbin.com/iemv">https://termbin.com/iemv</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="182952"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#182952" label="[18:29:52]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">script-generator and configurator are bypassed</span></div><div class="line" id="185437"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185437" label="[18:54:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">good to know a recent live-bootstrap is confirmed to succeed</span></div><div class="line" id="223728"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223728" label="[22:37:28]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap dies almost immediately with no error w/ latest M2libc though</span></div><div class="line" id="223733"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223733" label="[22:37:33]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">reverting worked however.</span></div><div class="line" id="223741"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223741" label="[22:37:41]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">FYI for anyone working on it</span></div><div class="line" id="223849"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223849" label="[22:38:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: just m2libc?</span></div><div class="line" id="223853"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223853" label="[22:38:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or the whole stage0-posix?</span></div><div class="line" id="223921"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223921" label="[22:39:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is this syntax error in aarch64 files <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/66/files">https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/66/files</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="223928"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223928" label="[22:39:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not sure if it will affect x86 build though</span></div><div class="line" id="223935"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223935" label="[22:39:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: what's the error by the way?</span></div><div class="line" id="224025"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224025" label="[22:40:25]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Error 02 trying to configure musl-1.1.24</span></div><div class="line" id="224033"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224033" label="[22:40:33]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but no actual output beyond that</span></div><div class="line" id="224121"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224121" label="[22:41:21]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I am not using stage0-posix though. Just live-bootstap with no submodules, dropping in my own hex0-mescc-tools-extra toolchain (which was built with latest m2libc)</span></div><div class="line" id="224153"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224153" label="[22:41:53]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then I just checkout M2libc in place and skip straight to build steps</span></div><div class="line" id="224333"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224333" label="[22:43:33]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Only thing I changed to get my build past the first musl pass was downgrade M2libc from 27ab5de4247e8b00459b2b21109109db8eb4d643 to 3a700010872697c4be9e3fab3cf707fce706741e</span></div><div class="line" id="224506"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224506" label="[22:45:06]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that means no tar symlink support, but nothing in the live-bootstrap source actually needs it, so not a blocker, but probably needs some integration testing.</span></div><div class="line" id="224537"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224537" label="[22:45:37]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">With my updated containerfile approach it can build in under 2h, so a good candidate for free CI service</span></div><div class="line" id="224610"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224610" label="[22:46:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">strange M2libc should have no effect so late...</span></div><div class="line" id="224700"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224700" label="[22:47:00]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well musl is built with tcc, so maybe m2libc impacted tcc in some way that broke the musl build</span></div><div class="line" id="224718"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224718" label="[22:47:18]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I assume tcc must build with m2libc</span></div><div class="line" id="224903"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224903" label="[22:49:03]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">May try swapping it back and debug deeper once I have this PR landed/working/verified</span></div><div class="line" id="230711"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#230711" label="[23:07:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no, tcc is built with meslibc</span></div><div class="line" id="230726"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#230726" label="[23:07:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and rebuilt quite a few times during bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="233031"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233031" label="[23:30:31]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">FYI PJ on the stagex team was able to get 386 go 1.23 built against musl and tcc very early in the live-bootstrap flow</span></div><div class="line" id="233037"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233037" label="[23:30:37]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That might unlock the removal of a lot of other steps</span></div><div class="line" id="233049"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233049" label="[23:30:49]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">go has a huge standard library</span></div><div class="line" id="233349"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#233349" label="[23:33:49]">&lt;rkeene&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas, <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/66">https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/66</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="233418"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233418" label="[23:34:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: ^^</span></div><div class="line" id="233533"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#233533" label="[23:35:33]">&lt;rkeene&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I also added some tests for tar here: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/mescc-tools-extra/pull/26/files">https://github.com/oriansj/mescc-tools-extra/pull/26/files</a>     some of the test relies on GNU tar, but it could be changed to not</span></div><div class="line" id="233656"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#233656" label="[23:36:56]">&lt;rkeene&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'll probably make some additional changes to tar, based on lrvick's note that it has problem untar'ing some files</span></div><br /></div></body></html>